"use strict";(self.webpackChunktie_md_website=self.webpackChunktie_md_website||[]).push([["607"],{7735(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"introducing-tie-md","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/introducing-tie-md","editUrl":"https://github.com/la-rebelion/tie.md-website/edit/main/blog/2026-11-09-why-tie-md.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-09-why-tie-md.mdx","title":"Introducing tie.md: a visible knot for specification-driven work","description":"Why we are building a local-first visual control plane around OpenSpec, coding agents, reusable skills, and Git.","date":"2026-11-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"tie-md","permalink":"/blog/tags/tie-md"},{"inline":true,"label":"openspec","permalink":"/blog/tags/openspec"},{"inline":true,"label":"agents","permalink":"/blog/tags/agents"},{"inline":true,"label":"local-first","permalink":"/blog/tags/local-first"}],"readingTime":1.47,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"La Rebelion Labs","title":"Building tools for independent makers","url":"https://rebelion.la","key":"la_rebelion_labs","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"introducing-tie-md","title":"Introducing tie.md: a visible knot for specification-driven work","authors":["la_rebelion_labs"],"tags":["tie-md","openspec","agents","local-first"],"description":"Why we are building a local-first visual control plane around OpenSpec, coding agents, reusable skills, and Git."},"unlisted":false},"content":"The modern development stack has plenty of powerful parts: specifications in Markdown, Git history, reusable agent instructions, focused skills, and coding agents that can implement real work.\\n\\nThe hard part is remembering how those parts fit together when you return tomorrow.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Great tools, loose ends\\n\\nAn OpenSpec change may have a proposal, design, specifications, tasks, validation output, an assigned agent, a handful of skills, changed files, tests, and delivery evidence. Each item is inspectable. The whole picture is not.\\n\\nThat gap creates familiar questions:\\n\\n- Which changes are actually ready?\\n- What is blocked, and by what evidence?\\n- Which agent is allowed to act?\\n- What changed on disk\u2014not just in the agent\'s summary?\\n- Which requirements are built, tested, passed, or delivered?\\n\\ntie.md is an attempt to answer those questions without moving the work into another proprietary system.\\n\\n## A control plane, not a replacement\\n\\nOpenSpec remains authoritative. Markdown and YAML remain reviewable. Git remains Git. Agents keep their own interfaces. tie.md reads that world, presents it through a familiar board and change workspace, and places an explicit human decision before consequential actions.\\n\\nThe bow-tie mark tells the same story: the specification wing on the left, the agent wing on the right, and the person\u2014the knot\u2014in the middle.\\n\\n## Starting deliberately small\\n\\nThe MVP targets one repository, one operator, and one mutating agent run at a time. It focuses on safe authoring, explicit assignments, supervised execution, and a bidirectional requirements traceability matrix.\\n\\nThe longer direction includes isolated parallel workspaces, agent teams, verification gates, scripts, MCP inventory, and Git-host integration. Those are directions, not promises. The invariant is more important: people approve consequential transitions, and the evidence remains inspectable.\\n\\nDevelopment is happening in the open at the [tie.md repository](https://github.com/la-rebelion/tie.md)."}]}}')}}]);